u have to  counterbalance it with some other term  that prevents just driving everything  down  and for us that's the data term in fact  in the paper we actually  do present kind of a family of methods  and that family is defined  by different objectives from you  different objectives for the adversarial  distribution  so the version that results in this a  kind of elegant expectation under mu  minus expectation under  um uh data thing actually has an entropy  regularizer on mu  you could imagine a cross entropy  regularizing you could imagine some  other regularization term and those  would all yield slightly different  algorithms  um i think we'll take oh  go ahead leon sorry yeah i'll just say  thanks  i'll do them in order in fact so there  was a question with maybe you can take  two more questions there was a question  from zhao how on yao on the on the q a  uh and i think this is actually also  maybe a question for the  the panel modulated discussion later um  are there policy-grade  algorithms that could be used sergey  here instead of q learning  yeah yeah there are um i'm sure emma can  say a lot more about this  uh because she's actually done actual  work on this but  uh yeah in fact many of the classic  algorithms for this are policy grading  algorithms with some  variant of important sampling and  there's a lot of really fascinating  analysis that we can do in terms of you  know sample complexity and  guarantees for those kinds of methods so  i completely skipped over that in my  talk because i have 20 minutes and i  can't cover everything but that's that's  a huge part of the literature  thank you very much emma i'm looking  forward to your answer maybe at 11 30.  um one last question then we'll take a  break uh recover from zoom fatigue a  little bit  uh this is from chava who is asking uh  how much does this idea of stitching  rely on a markov assumption yeah um  so since this is the last question i'm  gonna take a little bit of time to  answer  probably because i think this gets at  some fairly deep uh  issues that i that i you know i just  want to mention in regard to offline rls  so the short answer is yes it does  the long answer is when you take a bunch  of data  that shows behaviors with different  levels of optimality different returns  and you're asking to do better than the  best thing in the data set there must be  some structure that you can exploit  if there's no structure then the best  you can do is just pick the max  so what is the structure that we  exploited offline rl was it's the  temporal structure  in fact if we make no further assumption  the temporal structure is kind of all  we've got  so we need to have some kind of  structure which means that we need to  have the markov property  to hope to do better than the best thing  in the data set  uh if you have observations if you're in  a pump dp setting well go and learn your  state space  so that you do have a mark of the markov  property and then you can do better  that makes sense well thank you very  much sergey thank you all for this  first uh for first three talks and  accommodating the virtual schedule  uh this is a phenomenal start to a  workshop which  uh which promises great things um so on  this note we're going to take a half  hour break  to be back at um struggling with time  zones here to be back at 1 30 uh  eastern time or 10 30 pacific time  um for one more talk no i'm getting this  wrong you're right um we're going to be  back at 2 p.m eastern time  uh an 11 pacific time and um  for one more talk by ophea nahum and  then we will have a discussion  uh at 11 30. so thank you all  for this the morning morning talks and  i'll see you in half an hour
 

